.TH FSTACK 1 "November 2026" "fstack 1.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fstack, npush, npop, ncd, nstack, ndrop, nclear, nopen, nhere, nhelp \- push and pop folders on the Nautilus folder stack from bash
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B npush
.RI [ dir ]
.br
.B npop
.br
.B ncd
.RI [ n ]
.br
.B nstack
.br
.B ndrop
.I n
.br
.B nclear
.br
.B nopen
.RI [ n ]
.br
.B nhere
.RI [ dir ]
.br
.B nhelp
.RI [ command ]
.PP
.B fstack
.RB [ push
.RI [ dir ]
.RB | " pop " | " peek " | " get "
.I n
.RB | " drop "
.I n
.RB | " list " | " open "
.RI [ n ]
.RB | " clear " | " help "
.RI [ command ]
.RB | " version " ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The Nautilus folder stack is a list of folders that works like the shell's
.BR pushd / popd ,
shared between the GNOME Files app (Nautilus) and your bash prompt.
There is only one stack.
The
.B folder_stack.py
Nautilus extension shows it in the right-click
.B "Folder Stack"
menu and as sidebar bookmarks marked with \(lq\[u1F4CC]\(rq, which also appear
in GTK Open and Save dialogs.
The commands described here work on the same stack from a terminal.
A folder pushed in either place can be popped from the other.
.PP
Entry 0 is the top of the stack.
Pushing a folder that's already on the stack moves it to the top.
Removing a \[u1F4CC] bookmark from the Nautilus sidebar also removes that folder
from the stack.
.PP
The
.B n\&...
commands are bash functions defined in
.IR fstack.bash .
They exist because a program can't change its parent shell's directory, but a
function can.
Each one calls the
.B fstack
program, which you can also use directly in scripts.
.SH SHELL COMMANDS
.TP
.BR npush " [\fIdir\fR]"
Push
.I dir
(default: the current directory) onto the stack.
Your shell's directory doesn't change.
Same as
.BR "fstack push" .
.TP
.B npop
Remove the top folder from the stack and
.B cd
into it.
If the stack is empty, it fails and your directory doesn't change.
.TP
.BR ncd " [\fIn\fR]"
.B cd
to entry
.I n
(default 0) and leave it on the stack.
.TP
.B nstack
List the stack, top first, with each entry's position number.
.TP
.BI ndrop " n"
Remove entry
.IR n .
.TP
.B nclear
Empty the stack.
Bookmarks that aren't \[u1F4CC] stack bookmarks aren't touched.
.TP
.BR nopen " [\fIn\fR]"
Open entry
.I n
(default 0) in a Nautilus window.
.TP
.BR nhere " [\fIdir\fR]"
Open
.I dir
(default: the current directory) in Nautilus.
.TP
.BR nhelp " [\fIcommand\fR]"
Show an overview of all the commands, or help for one of them.
.PP
Every command accepts
.B \-h
or
.BR \-\-help .
Tab completion offers folders for
.BR npush " and " nhere ,
and valid stack positions for
.BR ncd ", " ndrop " and " nopen .
.SH FSTACK SUBCOMMANDS
Paths are printed to standard output.
Confirmations and errors go to standard error, so command substitution
captures only the path.
.TP
.BR push " [\fIdir\fR]"
Push
.I dir
(default:
.BR . ).
.TP
.B pop
Remove the top entry and print its path.
.TP
.BR peek ", " top
Print the top entry's path without removing it.
.TP
.BI get " n"
Print entry
.IR n 's
path without removing it.
.TP
.BI drop " n"
Remove entry
.IR n .
.TP
.BR list ", " ls
Print the numbered stack, with home-folder paths shortened to ~.
This is the default when no subcommand is given.
.TP
.BR open " [\fIn\fR]"
Open entry
.I n
(default 0) in Nautilus.
.TP
.B clear
Empty the stack.
.TP
.BR help " [\fIcommand\fR]"
Show help.
.I command
may be a subcommand or a shell command such as
.BR npush .
.TP
.BR version ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The stack was empty, there was no entry with the given number, or the
folder doesn't exist.
.TP
.B 2
Unknown command or bad usage.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/nautilus-folder-stack/state.json
The stack and folder history, shared with the Nautilus extension.
.TP
.I ~/.config/gtk-3.0/bookmarks
GTK bookmarks.
Stack entries are the lines whose label starts with \[u1F4CC].
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/nautilus-folder-stack/fstack.bash
The shell functions, sourced from
.IR ~/.bashrc .
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/nautilus-python/extensions/folder_stack.py
The Nautilus extension.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR XDG_DATA_HOME ", " XDG_CONFIG_HOME
If set, these replace
.I ~/.local/share
and
.IR ~/.config .
.SH EXAMPLES
Keep a project folder handy while you work elsewhere:
.PP
.RS
.nf
cd ~/school/physics/lab-report
npush
cd ~/Downloads
cp data.csv "$(fstack peek)"
npop                      # back in lab-report
.fi
.RE
.PP
Push a folder in Nautilus (right-click \(-> Folder Stack \(-> Push This Folder),
then pick it up in the terminal:
.PP
.RS
.nf
nstack
ncd 0
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR nautilus (1),
.BR bash (1)
(see
.BR pushd ", " popd ", " dirs )
